. *********************************
. *** Apply sample restrictions ***
. *********************************
. 
. * We estimate the following regressions using a sample restricted to responde
> nts with exactly one child.
. * Note: There was a typo in the preliminary Stata code provided in the PAP. A
> lthough the verbal description 
. * of the sample restriction was correct, the code mistakenly included `parity
> 2==1`, which would select 
. * respondents with at least two children—contradicting the intended analytic 
> sample.
. 
. 
. keep if parity2!=1 // Hypotheses 1, 2, 3, 4
(10,999 observations deleted)

. 
. * Restrict the analyses to cohabiting couples 
. keep if cohab==1 // Hypothesis 3
(7,503 observations deleted)

. 
. keep if !missing(daughters, female, syear, educ, birthyear, phrf) & phrf!=0
(13,661 observations deleted)

. keep if !missing(workhours) | !missing(housework)
(0 observations deleted)

. 
. count
  3,065

. 
. *********************************
. *********************************
. 
.          *HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H3 
. 
.         *********************************************************************
> *****************************************************************************
> ***************
.         *  We assume that gender-specific causal effects of having a daughter
>  are identified wihin strata defined by gender [female], cohort [birthyear] a
> nd education [educ] 
.         * in a sample of parents of a single child (see PAP)
.         * !! NOTE THIS IS THE AVERAGE CAUSAL EFFECT IN THE POPULATION OF PARE
> NTS OF A SINGLE CHILD !!
.         * We further assume that hours spent on labor [pgtatzeit] and housewo
> rk [pli0043_h] are suitable behavioral measures related to gender equality,
.         * at least among people living with a partner (hypothesis 3): 
.         * More time spent on labor and less time spent on housework is more l
> ikely to reflect gender equal behavior for women;
.         * less time spent on labor and more time spent on housework is more l
> ikely to reflect gender equal behavior for men.
.         * Accordingly, we run a seemingly unrelated linear regression analysi
> s with workhours [pgtatzeit] and housework [pli0043_h] as dependent variables
.         * and the dummy variable daughter as independent variable in a sample
>  of respondents with a single child (parity2!=1) living with a partner (cohab
> ==1)
.         * We apply weights cross-sectionally calibrated weights (phrf)
.         * To account for non-independence of observatons, we cluster standard
>  errors at the household level
.         * The null we test against is that the effect of having daughters is 
> >=0 on workours for men and on housework for women and
.         * <=0 on workhours for men and on housework for women.
.         * I.e., we have to consider a one-sided test adjusted for multiple te
> sting
.         * In practice, we multiply the first to estimates by -1 and test all 
> against >=0
.         * Since the used method provides a joint confidence band, this is equ
> al to assessing whether there is at least 1 significant effect
.         * We report all coefficients and statistics but only export the resul
> t with the smallest p-value to comply to the provided template
.         * !! NOTE THAT AT AN A PRIORI CONFIDENCE LEVEL OF 0.05 WITHOUT PREDER
> MINED SIGN OF THE EFFECT, THE CONFIDENCE LEVEL BECOMES 0.025
.         *********************************************************************
> *****************************************************************************
> ***************
.         * The below code follows the conception outlined in the PAP
.         * There is one deviation, which reflects perceived risk (2) already a
> nticipated in the PAP:
.         * as full stratification was not possible, we had to impose some cons
> traints
.         * We decided to only include interactions of daughters x female and a
> ll pairwise and triple interactions of these with the additional variables in
>  the model
.         * We refrained from more refined methods to determine the functional 
> form on a 5% subsample.
.         * We considered switching to a matching or IPW-weighting based approa
> ch but abandonded the idea in compliance with out PAP - but note that our app
> roach would
.         * call for sensitivity analysis w.r.t. to functional form assumptions
>  when applied to the full estimation sample.
.         * Note that the sample restriction is already implemented in the data
>  preparation and therefore omitted for redundancy
.         *********************************************************************
> *****************************************************************************
> ***************
. 
.         *obtain point esttimates for daughter effects on both outcomes
.         regress workhours (daughters##female)##(syear educ c.birthyear) [iw=p
> hrf]

